## 12. Streaming Response Latency & Time-to-First-Token (TTFT) Optimization

### 12.1 The Psychology of Perceived System Latency
In interactive conversational applications, human perception of latency follows strict psychological thresholds defined by Jakob Nielsen:
* **0.1 seconds (100 ms)**: Feels instantaneous. The user senses no lag.
* **1.0 second (1,000 ms)**: The user notices the delay, but their stream of thought remains uninterrupted.
* **10 seconds (10,000 ms)**: The limit of user attention. The user abandons the application or assumes the system has crashed.

A traditional blocking API request that generates 500 tokens over 6 seconds triggers cognitive friction and user dissatisfaction. 
By contrast, **Server-Sent Events (SSE) Streaming** emits the first token in **450 milliseconds** (Time-to-First-Token or TTFT), keeping the user engaged as text unfolds dynamically at natural reading speeds (30 to 50 tokens per second).


